.Dd November 23, 2026
.Dt LIBCUDART-STUB 3
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm libcudart-stub
.Nd linkable, non-executing cuda-native runtime stub
.Sh LIBRARY
The SDK tree contains
.Pa lib64/libcudart.so.13
and the relative symbolic link
.Pa lib64/libcudart.so .
Link with
.Fl L Ns Ar sdk/lib64 Fl l Ns Cm cudart ;
set a runtime search path when the SDK is not in the system library path.
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In cuda_runtime_api.h
.Ft cudaError_t
.Fn cudaLaunchKernel "const void *func" "dim3 grid" "dim3 block" "void **args" "size_t shared_mem" "cudaStream_t stream"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
library supplies the launch and registration entry points that clang 23 emits
for CUDA C++.
It allows a complete clang compile and link without an NVIDIA runtime.
It never submits work to a GPU.
.Pp
.Fn cudaLaunchKernel
always returns
.Dv cudaErrorNotSupported ,
whose numeric value is 801.
The function pointer, launch dimensions, arguments, shared-memory size and
stream are deliberately unused.
.Pp
For a kernel launch written with the triple-angle-bracket syntax, clang first
calls
.Fn __cudaPushCallConfiguration ,
then the generated device stub.
The device stub calls
.Fn __cudaPopCallConfiguration ,
then
.Fn cudaLaunchKernel .
The saved grid, block, shared-memory size and stream are thread-local, so the
push and pop operations on different threads do not share a launch
configuration.
Both configuration functions return zero.
.Pp
.Fn __cudaRegisterFatBinary
checks that the clang wrapper magic is
.Li 0x466243b1
and that its data is a cuda-native fatbin container.
The container carries its own length, but a length is not a bound: the bytes
the pointer names may be shorter than the container claims.
The registration bounds every read by the object that holds the pointer.
It finds that object with
.Xr dl_iterate_phdr 3 :
the
.Va PT_LOAD
segment that contains the pointer ends where the readable bytes of that
object end, and the registration reads no byte past that end.
The bound is never more than 16 MiB.
A pointer that is in no loaded object is refused, and so is a container that
claims more bytes than that bound allows.
A program that holds a container in a mapping of its own, which is in no
loaded object, can register that mapping with the reader; the bound is then
the end of the registered mapping.
A registration never widens the bound of a pointer that a loaded object
already bounds: the loaded object is the authority, and a registration only
answers for a pointer that is in no loaded object at all.
The registration validates the whole of the container it accepted.
It counts the members, reports that count in the optional trace and allocates
a separate record for each valid fatbin.
The record retains copied kernel names so unregister can trace and release each one.
A bad wrapper magic, bad data or a container that fails a check writes one
.Ql libcudart-stub:
diagnostic to standard error and returns
.Dv NULL .
If the registration record cannot be allocated, the diagnostic instead ends in
.Ql host memory exhausted ,
which states the host-side cause.
.Pp
.Fn __cudaRegisterFunction
adds the device kernel name to the record, and
.Fn __cudaUnregisterFatBinary
releases its names and storage.
The library also exports
.Fn __cudaRegisterFatBinaryEnd ,
.Fn __cudaRegisterVar ,
.Fn __cudaRegisterManagedVar ,
.Fn __cudaRegisterSurface
and
.Fn __cudaRegisterTexture .
These entry points retain no additional registration state.
No registration function makes a driver call.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn cudaLaunchKernel
always returns 801.
The launch configuration functions return zero.
.Fn __cudaRegisterFatBinary
returns a per-fatbin handle or
.Dv NULL
for bad input or a host-memory allocation failure.
.Fn __cudaRegisterFunction
returns zero, or 1 if host-side name storage cannot be allocated.
These return values describe only the stub's host-side bookkeeping; none
reports successful device execution.
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width CUDA_NATIVE_STUB_TRACE
.It Ev CUDA_NATIVE_STUB_TRACE
When set exactly to
.Ql 1 ,
each fatbin/function registration, registration end/unregister and kernel
launch writes one JSON object on its own standard-error line.
When set to any other nonempty value, the JSON lines are appended to the file
named by that value.
If the file cannot be opened, one diagnostic is written to standard error per
process and the host program continues without the trace.
The diagnostic states that tracing is disabled and advises setting
.Ev CUDA_NATIVE_STUB_TRACE
to a writable path or unsetting it.
The event shapes are:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
{"event":"register_fatbin","members":1}
{"event":"register_function","name":"_Z4vaddPKfS0_Pfi"}
{"event":"register_fatbin_end"}
{"event":"launch","status":801}
{"event":"unregister_fatbin"}
{"event":"unregister_function","name":"_Z4vaddPKfS0_Pfi"}
.Ed
The member count and kernel name reflect the actual registration.
Every object validates against
.Pa share/cuda-native/schema/cuda-native-stub-trace-1.schema.json .
When the variable is unset or empty, these calls write no trace.
It is also ignored whenever
.Fn issetugid
reports a set-id process.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Compile and link a CUDA source with an SDK in
.Pa obj/cuda-native :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
/usr/local/llvm23/bin/clang++ -x cuda \e
    --cuda-path=obj/cuda-native --cuda-gpu-arch=sm_121 kernel.cu \e
    -Lobj/cuda-native/lib64 -Wl,-rpath,"$PWD/obj/cuda-native/lib64" -lcudart
.Ed
.Pp
A resulting launch returns 801 rather than running the kernel.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr cuda-objdump 1 ,
.Xr nvfatbin 1 ,
.Xr nvptxas 1 ,
.Xr nvfatbin 5 ,
.Xr cuda-native-toolchain 7
.Sh CAVEATS
This is an intentionally small runtime stub for clang's generated host calls,
not the CUDA runtime or a compatible implementation of its general API.
Memory allocation, copies, streams, events and other runtime services are not
implemented.
Every launch fails with 801.
The real libcudart is Track #2430.
There is no
.Nm libcuda ,
no device execution and no interoperability with an NVIDIA runtime.
The registration and launch signatures are derived from clang's local CUDA
runtime wrapper and the LLVM IR clang generates, not from NVIDIA toolkit
binaries or libraries.
